        <body>
            <div xml:id="d312">

                <pb facs="0504" n="410" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg410"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.945/1213a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-09-11T16:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 11,
                                1924—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>152. Associated Press despatch from Tokyo published this morning states: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The strong frank tone of Ambassador Hanihara’s immigration note to the
                            American Government in which the term ‘grave consequences’ was used was
                            due to the insistence of Secretary of State Charles E. Hughes who wanted
                            a note ‘with teeth’ Bishop Charles F. Reifsnider said today in an
                            address before the Tokyo Rotary Club”.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>When this was shown to me I said that it was absurd and that there was no truth
                    in it. I did not discuss the despatch as it seemed unworthy of further notice. I
                    send this for your information and guidance and not for the purpose of provoking
                    discussion.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
